rules_text:
{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially during rising regimes. Apply an upward level shift (≈ +0.3 to +0.8 €/t near-term; larger for mid/long horizons when trend is up).",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: after a local low in history, the model turns up too slowly and misses the subsequent rally. Add a trend-following component: if last 5–10 days slope > 0, increase days 8–25 forecasts progressively (ramp up correction with horizon).",
    "Horizon-specific error grows with time: small errors in days 1–5, but underprediction widens materially in days ~10–25 during upswings (often +2 to +4 €/t). Use a horizon-weighted correction (minimal for days 1–3, moderate for 4–10, strongest for 11–25).",
    "Upside tail suppression: peaks are clipped (model rarely reaches the true highs during rallies). If forecast path has capped highs while recent volatility is elevated, widen upside by adding a volatility-scaled uplift to local maxima (e.g., +0.5× recent 10d stdev).",
    "Late-horizon reversal risk: when the model finally projects a sharp late jump (days ~26–30), it can overshoot/arrive too late relative to reality (timing error). Smooth and pull forward: shift part of late-horizon increases into mid-horizon (days 15–25) and dampen the last 3–5 days uplift."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift (≈ +0.5 to +1.5 €/t early horizon; ≈ +2 to +4 €/t mid/late horizon).",
    "Trend/acceleration underestimation: when the recent history turns upward (post-dip rebound), the model reacts too slowly and misses the subsequent rally. Add a momentum term: if last 5–10 days slope > 0, increase forecasts progressively with horizon (larger uplift after day ~7).",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: forecast error increases with lead time (near-term errors small; 2–4 week horizon errors large). Use a ramping correction: uplift grows roughly linearly with horizon (e.g., +0.05 to +0.15 €/t per forecast day).",
    "Underpricing of high-price regime: once spot moves into ~70–73 range, the model mean-reverts too low (anchors around high-60s). Impose regime adjustment: if last close > 70, shift the whole path upward and reduce mean-reversion strength.",
    "Insufficient spike capture: during sharp upward moves, model peaks are too low and too early (damped amplitude). Inflate volatility/peak amplitude conditional on recent large positive returns (e.g., if any of last 3 daily returns > +1.5, add extra +1 to +2 €/t around days 5–15).",
    "Late-horizon overshoot risk after extreme late spikes (seen in one case): if model projects a steep late surge (> +3 €/t over 5 days) without matching momentum in the last 10 days of history, cap the late-horizon slope (soft ceiling) to avoid unrealistic blow-off tops."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model underpredicts the true path in rising regimes. Apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger from ~day 10 onward).",
    "Trend/lag error: forecasts react too slowly to upside momentum (true series ramps from high-60s to low-70s while model stays ~68–70). Add a momentum term based on the last 5–10 day slope; if slope > 0, tilt the whole forecast upward, especially mid/long horizon.",
    "Horizon-specific underestimation: largest errors occur around days ~12–25 where true outcomes are ~2–4 EUR above forecast. Use a piecewise correction: +~0.5–1 EUR (days 1–7), +~2–3 EUR (days 8–20), +~1–2 EUR (days 21–30), conditional on positive recent trend.",
    "Insufficient amplitude on rallies: model captures direction but not magnitude during breakouts to 71–73. When price breaks above recent 20–30d range/high, increase projected rally amplitude (scale deviations from baseline by >1).",
    "Over-mean-reversion after strength: model tends to pull back toward ~69–70 even when the true series stays elevated. Reduce mean-reversion strength when spot is above the 20d moving average and the MA is rising."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias: model underpredicts during rising regimes (true often +1 to +3 EUR above forecast for ~day 5–25). Apply an upward level shift when the last 5–10 days show a positive slope.",
    "Trend/lag error: forecasts react too slowly to breakouts; peaks occur ~1–3 days later in the model and with smaller amplitude. If price breaks above the 10–15d range, accelerate the path upward (front-load gains).",
    "Underestimated volatility on upside: during rallies, the model caps highs (misses 72–73 area) and keeps forecasts clustered ~68–70. Increase upside variance/allow higher maxima when recent realized range expands.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot after regime change: when the true market turns down (late July/early Aug), the model sometimes stays too high or predicts a delayed spike. Add stronger mean-reversion / downward adjustment beyond ~20–30d when momentum turns negative.",
    "Horizon structure: near-term (1–5d) errors are smaller; mid-horizon (7–20d) shows largest underprediction in uptrends; far-horizon (20–30d) tends to revert too much or overshoot depending on the latest swing. Use regime-dependent corrections by horizon (uptrend: add more mid-horizon uplift; downtrend: add more far-horizon decay)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias beyond ~1 week: the model systematically under-forecasts during sustained uptrends (true path runs ~+1 to +4 EUR above forecast from ~day 7–25 in most examples). Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small at days 1–3, larger from days 7–20).",
    "Trend/lag error: forecasts follow the direction but with insufficient slope (lagging rallies). When the last 5–10 days of history show a clear upswing, steepen the forecast trajectory (add positive drift) rather than reverting toward the recent mean.",
    "Underestimation of post-breakout persistence: after a jump to the low-70s, the model expects quick mean reversion, but realized prices stay elevated for 1–3 weeks. Reduce mean-reversion strength after upside breakouts; keep prices higher for longer.",
    "Horizon-dependent sign flip near the end: at longer horizons (~day 25–30) the model often stays too high when the realized series starts to roll over (late-period drop toward mid/upper-60s). Introduce stronger late-horizon mean reversion / downside adjustment when recent history shows weakening momentum.",
    "Volatility compression: the model’s amplitude is too small in strong moves (misses peaks around 72–73). Inflate forecast variance (or widen bands) and allow higher highs during bullish regimes; conversely, allow sharper late pullbacks when momentum turns."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent underprediction in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show a clear uptrend, add an upward bias to the forecast path (model is typically ~+1 to +3 EUR/t too low, especially at horizons ~5–20 days).",
    "Trend/level lag: the model reacts too slowly to regime shifts (post-breakout). Apply a faster level adjustment by anchoring early-horizon forecasts closer to the latest spot (increase weight on last observed price vs. model path).",
    "Peak underestimation after upside spikes: following sharp upward moves, the model systematically underestimates the subsequent plateau/secondary highs. Add extra uplift for days 3–15 after an upside spike rather than assuming quick normalization.",
    "Over-optimistic late-horizon in downshifts: when the realized market turns down (or volatility rises), the model’s far-horizon tail stays too high. Impose stronger mean-reversion downward beyond ~20 days if recent returns turn negative.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias shape: apply a hump-shaped correction—largest positive adjustment mid-horizon (≈days 5–20), smaller adjustment near day 1–3, and neutral-to-negative adjustment at the far tail (≈days 20–30) depending on recent trend sign."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias at short-to-mid horizons (≈D+1 to D+20): model underpredicts during rising regimes; apply an upward level adjustment of roughly +1 to +3 €/t when the last 5–10 days show positive momentum.",
    "Trend/lag error: forecasts respond too slowly to sharp up-moves (miss the jump and subsequent elevated plateau). If a 1–3 day rally >~2 €/t occurs, shift the next ~10–15 forecast days upward and steepen the near-term slope (don’t fade immediately).",
    "Systematic underestimation of peaks: during strong rallies the model caps too low (misses 72–73 area in examples). Add a convexity/peak premium in the first half of the horizon when volatility is elevated (e.g., widen upside by +1–2 €/t).",
    "Over-optimism at long horizons (≈D+25 to D+30): model often projects continued strength while realized prices mean-revert lower. Apply a downward taper to the tail (reduce last 5–7 days by ~2–6 €/t, stronger when the model tail is above recent 10-day mean).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: when recent prices have spiked up, the model extrapolates too high into the far tail; enforce mean reversion toward the 10–20 day average beyond ~3 weeks.",
    "Regime-switch miss (late sell-off): model fails to anticipate sharp drawdowns following elevated levels; if spot is above the 20-day mean by >~2 €/t and momentum turns negative, bias the entire path downward and accelerate the decline beyond ~D+15."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent underprediction in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show a clear upswing (positive slope), add an upward bias of ~+1.5 to +3.0 €/t to the forecast for the next ~1–15 days (model is consistently too low during rallies).",
    "Turning-point lag: after a sharp drop begins (regime shift from up to down), the model stays too high and too smooth. Apply a faster mean-reversion/downshift: subtract ~1–2 €/t for days ~10–30 when recent momentum flips negative (last 3–7 day return < 0).",
    "Over-optimism at long horizon after peaks: when recent prices are elevated (e.g., >20-day mean by ~1–2 std) the model projects continued strength, but realized paths revert lower by weeks 3–4. Damp long-horizon forecasts toward the 20–30 day mean (shrink deviations by ~30–50% for days 15–30).",
    "Volatility underestimation: the model’s path is too flat vs realized swings. Increase responsiveness by scaling forecast day-to-day changes up (e.g., multiply predicted daily deltas by ~1.2–1.4) but cap extremes to avoid runaway.",
    "Spike handling: after upside spikes, the model underreacts immediately (next ~1–7 days too low), but then overshoots persistence (weeks 3–4 too high). Use a two-stage adjustment: boost near-term (+1–3 €/t for days 1–7) and reduce medium/long-term (−1–3 €/t for days 15–30)."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime bias: when the last 5–10 days show a sustained upswing (higher highs / rising 10d MA), the model is systematically too low across the first ~15–20 forecast days. Apply an upward level shift of roughly +1 to +3 €/t (stronger in the first half of the horizon).",
    "Turning-point lag: the model reacts ~2–5 trading days late to sharp accelerations (breakouts). If the last 3–5 days’ return is strongly positive, steepen the near-term path (days 1–10) rather than keeping it flat.",
    "Post-peak crash underestimation: after a multi-week rally followed by a clear reversal (large negative daily moves / break below short MA), the model remains too high in the back half of the horizon (days ~15–30). Add stronger mean-reversion downward adjustment (often −2 to −6 €/t by day 30).",
    "Overshoot on renewed strength: in late examples the model projects continued strength into days 20–30 (sometimes >73–74) while realized prices mean-revert lower. Cap long-horizon upside by anchoring days 20–30 closer to the last observed price or 20d average (reduce slope).",
    "Horizon asymmetry: apply corrections that are front-loaded in rising regimes (boost days 1–15) and back-loaded in falling regimes (cut days 15–30). Avoid uniform shifts across all 30 days.",
    "Volatility compression: the model’s forecast range is too narrow during rallies (misses higher highs) but too wide on the upside late in the horizon after reversals. Increase near-term volatility allowance in breakouts; dampen long-horizon volatility via stronger pull-to-mean."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent underprediction in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show a clear upswing, add an upward level shift to the whole path (model is typically ~+1 to +3 EUR/t too low in the first half of the horizon).",
    "Turning-point lag: after an upside breakout (new 10–20 day high), the model reacts too slowly; front-load the adjustment (bigger correction in days 1–10 than days 20–30).",
    "Missed downside breaks: when a sharp sell-off begins (2–4 day drop), the model stays too high and forecasts a rebound that doesn’t materialize; apply a negative correction that increases with horizon (largest in days ~15–30).",
    "Over-optimism after peaks: following a local peak/spike, the model tends to project continued strength while outcomes mean-revert lower; cap post-peak forecasts and impose stronger mean reversion beyond ~10 trading days.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (days 1–7) bias is mostly ‘too low’ in rallies, but medium/long horizon (days 15–30) bias flips to ‘too high’ during drawdowns—use regime detection to switch the sign of correction by horizon.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are too smooth versus realized moves; widen expected range by increasing sensitivity to recent realized volatility (especially after regime shifts)."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime bias: when the recent 10–15d trend is strongly up, the model is systematically too low (underreacts to rallies). Apply an upward level shift of roughly +1 to +3 €/t for the first ~2 weeks in an upswing.",
    "Downturn miss: when a peak is followed by consecutive declines (local top in history), the model stays too high and too flat. Impose faster mean reversion downward: subtract ~2–5 €/t over horizons ~10–30d, with the largest cut after day ~15.",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: short horizon (1–7d) errors are modest; medium/long horizon (10–30d) shows persistent overestimation during sell-offs. Use a negative slope adjustment beyond ~day 10 when recent returns turn negative.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: after sharp upward spikes in history, the model extrapolates elevated levels for too long, but realized prices revert and can break lower. Cap post-spike forecasts and fade them: reduce forecast levels progressively with horizon (stronger fade after day ~10).",
    "Volatility compression: the model forecasts are overly smooth versus realized moves (both up and down). Increase responsiveness to recent momentum for 1–10d, but increase mean-reversion strength for 10–30d to avoid persistent level errors."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime shift bias: the model is broadly too bullish. After ~day 10–15 it maintains prices in the low-70s while realized prices trend down into the mid-60s; apply a negative level shift to the whole path when recent momentum turns down.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: near-term (days 1–7) errors are small-to-moderate, but medium/long horizon (days 10–30) errors become strongly positive. Use an increasing downward correction with horizon (e.g., subtract more as lead time increases).",
    "Failure to capture drawdowns: when the last 5–10 days show weakening/rollover, the model still extrapolates prior strength. Add a mean-reversion term toward a trailing average (e.g., 10–20d) and dampen trend extrapolation when recent returns turn negative.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following a local spike/peak in history (around 71–73), the model forecasts continued elevated levels, but realized prices revert and then sell off. Cap post-spike forecasts and enforce faster reversion after large positive moves.",
    "Lag in turning points: the model reacts late to the transition from uptrend to downtrend (keeps predicting rising/flat while actual declines). Introduce a turning-point detector (e.g., short MA crossing below long MA) that flips the correction sign quickly.",
    "Volatility asymmetry: the model underestimates downside tail risk; realized paths show sharper drops than forecast. Add downside skew: when recent volatility rises, widen lower bands / apply extra negative adjustment."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime shift / level bias: when the last-30d history is in the high-60s/low-70s and the next month transitions into a selloff, the model stays anchored too high. Apply a downward level adjustment once a downside break occurs (i.e., after the first clear drop day, shift the whole path lower).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors grow with horizon. Days 1–7 are mildly low/close; days ~10–30 are systematically too high. Use an increasing negative correction with lead time (small at 1w, larger at 2–4w).",
    "Missed mean reversion after peaks: following a local spike/peak (e.g., >~72–73 in these samples), the model extrapolates strength instead of reverting. Impose mean reversion: cap upside continuation and pull forecasts back toward the recent 10–20d mean after a peak.",
    "Overshoot on late-horizon rallies: when the model projects a renewed rally in weeks 3–4, actual outcomes often continue drifting down. Damp trend components beyond ~15 trading days (reduce slope/volatility; bias toward flat-to-down).",
    "Underestimation of drawdown magnitude: during selloffs the model predicts a shallow decline while realized prices fall much further (into mid-60s/low-60s). Add a drawdown amplifier once a downtrend is established (e.g., if 5–10d momentum is negative, increase the expected decline rate and lower terminal level)."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime shift bias: when the recent history is in the high-60s/low-70s and momentum turns down, the model stays anchored too high. Apply a downward level shift of ~4–8 €/t across the 2–4 week horizon once a drawdown starts (i.e., after the local peak).",
    "Horizon-dependent error: near-term (days 1–7) forecasts are slightly low during the final upswing (+0–1.5 €/t), but medium/long horizon (days 10–30) becomes strongly too high (+5–12 €/t). Use a correction that increases with horizon after a peak: subtract ~0–2 €/t (d1–7), ~3–6 €/t (d8–15), ~6–12 €/t (d16–30).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following a price spike/peak (e.g., >71–73), the model extrapolates elevated levels and misses subsequent sell-offs. If the last observed price is >2 €/t above the 10–20d average, cap the forecast path and force faster mean reversion (pull 50–70% back toward the 20d mean by day 15).",
    "Underestimation of mean reversion speed: true outcomes revert downward much faster than the model. Increase reversion speed whenever 5–10d momentum turns negative (e.g., 5d MA < 10d MA): blend forecasts toward the 20d mean with a weight that grows with horizon (e.g., 20% at d5 → 60% at d20).",
    "Trend-lag: the model reacts late to turning points (keeps rising/flat while realized starts falling). Use a turning-point trigger: if the last 3–5 realized closes are down and volatility is elevated, shift the entire forecast slope downward (impose a negative drift of ~0.2–0.4 €/t/day for the next 10–15 days)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Regime-shift/turning-point miss: when the last 5–10 days show a sharp selloff or break below the 20–30d mean, the model stays anchored to the prior uptrend and overpredicts for ~2–3 weeks. Apply a downward level shift early in the horizon.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are close in days 1–5 but become increasingly too high from ~day 7 onward (largest errors days 10–25). Use an error ramp (increasing negative correction with horizon).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following a local peak (e.g., >2–3 EUR jump in a few sessions), the model extrapolates strength; true outcomes mean-revert and often reverse. Cap upside continuation and force mean reversion toward the 20–30d average.",
    "Underestimates immediate continuation only at the very start of the reversal: in the first ~1–3 days after an upswing, the model can be slightly low, but then quickly becomes too high as the true series rolls over. Use a small short-horizon uplift only if momentum is strongly positive, then fade.",
    "Volatility/dispersion mismatch: the model path is too smooth and too high; true outcomes show larger downside tails. Add asymmetric volatility adjustment: widen bands and skew corrections negative when recent returns are negative.",
    "Mean level anchoring: when history contains a recent high plateau (70–74), the model anchors there even as the market transitions to mid-60s. Re-anchor forecasts to the most recent 5–10d mean rather than the 30d mean during drawdowns."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong positive level bias: model forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices (often by ~+2 to +8 EUR), especially after mid-August; apply a downward offset that increases with forecast horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: model extrapolates recent strength and misses regime shifts; when the last 5–10 days show weakening/volatility, damp the forecast slope (reduce day-to-day drift toward the recent trend).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following local highs (e.g., ~71–73), the model projects continued gains, but realized prices mean-revert and can sell off sharply; cap upside continuation and force partial mean reversion toward the 20–30d average after a spike.",
    "Horizon-specific error: near-term (days 1–5) errors are smaller, but medium/long horizon (days 10–30) errors blow out and stay too bullish; progressively shrink deviations from the current spot/short-term mean as horizon extends (e.g., stronger reversion weight beyond day ~7–10).",
    "Volatility underestimation on downside: realized paths show faster, deeper drawdowns than forecast; increase downside responsiveness by allowing larger negative moves when recent returns turn negative (asymmetric adjustment: stronger correction when momentum flips down)."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Short-horizon (t+1 to t+7) bias: forecasts are slightly too low in downtrends and early recoveries. Apply a small upward level shift (~+0.2 to +0.5 €/t) when the last 5–10 days show falling prices followed by stabilization (i.e., slope turning from negative to flat).",
    "Turning-point lag: the model tends to recognize reversals 1–3 days late (keeps projecting the prior drift). Heuristic: if the last 3 days are up after a multi-day decline, tilt the next 3–5 forecast days upward (reduce projected decline / add +0.3 to +0.8 €/t depending on recent volatility).",
    "Medium-horizon (t+10 to t+25) underestimation during rallies: when spot rebounds strongly (e.g., move from low 60s back to mid/high 60s), forecasts often cap the upside and stay below realized levels by ~1–3 €/t. Add an upside extension: if 10-day momentum > +2 €/t, raise days t+10..t+25 by ~+1.0 to +2.0 €/t.",
    "Late-horizon (t+20 to t+30) spike handling: forecasts sometimes overshoot downward (projecting drops to ~60) while outcomes revert upward (mid/high 60s). Impose mean-reversion anchoring: constrain the last-third of the path toward a rolling 20–30d mean (e.g., blend 50% forecast with 50% 20d mean) unless trend strength is extreme.",
    "Volatility damping around sharp jumps: after large up-moves in the true series, the model often underreacts (too smooth), then remains too low for several days. If a >2 €/t single-day jump occurs in history, increase the next 5–10 forecast days by ~+0.5 to +1.5 €/t and reduce any immediate projected pullback magnitude by ~25–40%.",
    "Amplitude bias: forecast paths are generally too low-variance versus realized outcomes (miss higher highs during rebounds). Apply a regime-based scaling: in high-volatility regimes (e.g., 10d realized vol above recent median), expand deviations from the mean by ~10–20%."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are close in the first ~1–7 days, but become increasingly too low from ~day 10 onward (systematic underprediction in rising regimes). Apply an upward drift correction that grows with horizon (e.g., +0.1 to +0.2 €/day after day 7, capped).",
    "Trend/lag error: model reacts too slowly to regime shifts (misses sustained rebounds). If the last 5–10 days show a positive slope, add a trend-following adjustment to the whole path (proportional to recent slope), especially for days 10–30.",
    "Spike underestimation: after sharp upside moves in the realized series, the model tends to fade them too quickly and undershoots subsequent levels. When a 1–3 day jump exceeds a threshold, reduce mean-reversion strength and lift days 5–20 by a fixed premium.",
    "Level anchoring too low: forecasts appear anchored to the recent local mean around low-60s even when the market transitions to mid/high-60s. Use a blended anchor: increase weight on the most recent 3–5 day average vs the 30-day mean when recent prices are accelerating upward.",
    "Late-horizon compression: forecast volatility is too low at 20–30d, missing higher terminal outcomes. Inflate the distribution/trajectory range (wider bands) and raise the terminal level when recent realized volatility has increased."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Uptrend underpricing (horizon 10–30d): when the recent 5–10d slope turns positive after a drawdown, the model lags and stays too low; apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small in week 1, larger by weeks 3–4).",
    "Spike/step-change lag: after a sharp upside break (e.g., +2 to +4 in 1–3 days), the model partially follows but remains below the new regime for ~1–2 weeks; add a catch-up term proportional to the size of the break, decaying over ~10 trading days.",
    "Over-mean-reversion in late horizon: beyond ~day 15 the model often pulls forecasts back toward ~63–66 even when realized prices persist in the high-60s; reduce mean-reversion strength when the last 20–30d range shifts upward (higher highs/higher lows).",
    "Down-move handling is better than up-move handling: early-horizon errors during declines are small, but during rebounds the model underestimates; use asymmetric correction—stronger positive adjustments than negative ones for the same recent momentum magnitude.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast paths are too smooth vs. realized outcomes (miss higher peaks); widen forecast distribution/allow higher amplitudes when recent realized volatility increases (e.g., 10d stdev rising)."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are close for days 1–10 but become increasingly too low from ~day 15 onward, especially in rising regimes (systematic underestimation of late-horizon levels). Apply an upward drift correction that increases with lead time (e.g., +0.5 to +1.0 €/t by day 10, +2 to +4 €/t by days 20–30 when trend is up).",
    "Trend/lag effect: the model reacts to upswings with a delay; it captures direction but not magnitude, leading to persistent negative errors during rallies. Use a momentum adjustment: if last 5–10 days return is positive, add a proportional uplift to the whole path (larger for longer horizons).",
    "Spike handling: after sharp upward moves in the realized series, the model mean-reverts too aggressively and misses the continuation (underpredicts post-spike plateau). Add a 'post-spike persistence' rule: if a 1–3 day jump > ~2 €/t occurred recently, reduce mean-reversion strength and shift forecasts upward for the next 2–3 weeks.",
    "Asymmetry: downside phases are forecast more accurately than upside phases; errors are predominantly negative during the Oct rally. Use regime detection (uptrend vs downtrend) and apply corrections mainly in uptrend regimes; keep minimal/no correction in downtrends.",
    "Level anchoring: forecasts appear anchored near ~62–66 even when the market transitions to ~68–70, indicating insufficient level adjustment. Add a level calibration: blend forecast with a rolling realized mean plus recent trend (e.g., 70% model + 30% [last 10d mean + slope*lead])."
  ]
}

{
  "rules": [
    "Uptrend underestimation: when the last 5–10 days show a clear rebound/rally, the model systematically underprices the next 10–30 days. Apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small at 1–5d, larger at 15–30d).",
    "Horizon-dependent negative bias: errors are modest in the first week but become increasingly negative beyond ~day 10, especially in rising regimes. Use a linear ramp correction (e.g., add progressively more from day 10 to day 30).",
    "Lag after regime change: following a local bottom and subsequent breakout, the forecast path reacts too slowly (trend lag). Increase slope/gradient of the forecast for ~1–2 weeks after a reversal signal.",
    "Missed spike magnitude: for strong upside moves in the realized path (late-horizon highs), the model captures direction but not amplitude. Add a volatility/momentum kicker when recent realized volatility or 3–5 day momentum is high.",
    "Over-smoothing/mean-reversion pull: forecasts revert toward the prior mean too aggressively, causing persistent underprediction during sustained climbs. Reduce mean-reversion strength when price is above a rising short-term moving average (e.g., 5d > 20d)."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially during rising regimes. Apply an upward level shift (larger for longer horizons).",
    "Trend/lag error: model underreacts to upside momentum—after a turn higher, it takes ~5–15 days to catch up. Add a momentum term: if last 5–10 days are up, lift the whole path, with increasing lift beyond day ~10.",
    "Horizon-dependent underestimation: errors widen with forecast horizon (days ~15–30 most affected). Use a ramping correction (small +0–1 EUR early, larger +2–5 EUR late, conditional on positive trend).",
    "Upside tail miss: model fails to capture late-horizon rallies/spikes (e.g., outcomes push to high-60s while forecasts stay mid-60s). Add a convexity kicker: when spot is above its 20–30d MA and MA slope > 0, increase upper quantiles/late-horizon levels.",
    "Too much mean-reversion after dips: following local lows, model expects reversion down/flat, but realized path rebounds more strongly. Reduce mean-reversion strength when price is below MA but MA slope is turning up."
  ]
}

{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially beyond ~day 10. Apply an upward level shift that increases with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger in days 10–30).",
